\viewkind4\uc1 \f1\fs20\b STUDY SHEET\b0\par
\f1\fs32\b Pursuing Justice\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Deuteronomy 16:18\u8211?21:9 | Haftarah: Isaiah 51:12\u8211?52:12 | Gospel: John 14:9\u8211?20\par
\f0\fs22\i In Shoftim all authority \u8212? judge, king, prophet, priest \u8212? is given by God, and precisely therefore bounded and testable. Around the image of the king who copies out the Torah by his own hand, \u8216?that his heart may not be lifted up,\u8217? the reading moves toward a God who protects life even in war, and toward Yeshua as the authority that does \u8216?not speak from Himself.\u8217?\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 This round fixes the production order: first observe, then the
axes as an open question, then read off the center of gravity, and only
then the thread. The directional test is the horizon, not the starting
point.

\par\b\fs24 0.1 Blind observation\b0\fs24\par

The parashah opens with an imperative of institutions: \u8216?Judges and
officials you shall appoint in all your gates\u8217? (16:18), and immediately
the loudest line of the whole: tzedek tzedek tirdof \u8212? \u8216?justice, justice
you shall pursue\u8217? (16:20). Then, block by block, the bearers of
authority and their limits: the judge (no bribe, no partiality), the
high court, the king (whom God chooses but who does not multiply
horses/wives/gold and writes out the Torah), the priests (whose
inheritance is the LORD), the ban on divination, the prophet like Moses
with his test, the cities of refuge, the witness rule and
proportionality, the laws of war, and the unsolved murder where the
elders wash their hands. A recurring tension: authority that is
commanded AND strictly bounded.

\par\b\fs24 0.2 The four axes as an open question\b0\fs24\par









\b Axis\b0 
\b What the text brings up\b0 
\b Weight\b0 




\b Source\b0 
The legitimacy of every office comes from God: the king whom He
chooses (17:15), the prophet whom He raises (18:15), the priest whose
inheritance is Himself (18:2). Illegitimate sources are rejected
(divination 18:10\u8211?14, the false prophet 18:20).
Dominant.


\b Coherence\b0 
The whole parashah is one system of limits on power: the king does
not lift his heart (17:20), the prophet is tested (18:22), two witnesses
not one (19:15), proportionality (19:21), careful investigation
(17:4).
Dominant \u8212? interlocks with Source.


\b Will\b0 
Present but bounded: pursue justice (16:20), establish institutions
(16:18), wash the hands over innocent blood (21:6).
Supporting.


\b Trajectory\b0 
\u8216?When you come into the land\u8217? (17:14); the future king.
Thin.


\b Reactivity\b0 
Mild: \u8216?not like the nations\u8217? who listen to soothsayers (18:9,14);
the king who \u8216?does not return to Egypt\u8217? (17:16).
Weak; not foregrounded.




\par\b\fs24 0.3 Read-off center of gravity\b0\fs24\par

Read off, not chosen: the parashah is about righteous,
bounded, legitimate authority \u8212? who may judge, rule, speak, and
by whose authority; and that every power stands under God and His Torah
and remains accountable. The two bearing axes are
\b Source\b0  (all authority comes from God) and
\b Coherence\b0  (and is therefore testable and bounded).
Backbone: tzedek tzedek tirdof (16:20) and the king-under-the-Torah
(17:18\u8211?20).

\par\b\fs24 0.4 Framing test\b0\fs24\par

Did this come from the observation? Yes \u8212? the density is overwhelming
(judges, court, king, priests, prophet, witnesses, cities of refuge,
war, unsolved murder); it is almost the whole parashah.
Two pitfalls, named honestly. One: I could bend this back toward the
directional rung (the false prophet and \u8216?you shall be blameless\u8217? are,
after all, about received versus grasped authority) \u8212? but that would
flatten the core; the text does not press on the weighing but on the
structure: how authority is instituted AND bounded. Two, more important:
last week (Re\u8217?eh) I landed on the Source\u8211?Will relation; I must not
impose that template now. Tested: the text stages the Source\u8211?Coherence
coupling itself \u8212? 17:18\u8211?20 binds the source (God chooses the king) to
the limit (he writes out the Torah), and 18:15\u8211?22 binds the prophet\u8217?s
legitimacy to the test. It is observation, not import.
Outcome: it does not land on the directional rung, nor on last week\u8217?s
Source\u8211?Will \u8212? it lands on \b Source\u8211?Coherence\b0 . That the
text brought me elsewhere is the proof that the protocol observes rather
than frames.

\par\b\fs28 \u167?1. The thread\b0\fs24\par

Pursuing justice \u8212? authority that is given, and therefore
bounded. All power in Shoftim is derived (the king, the
prophet, the priest receive their place from God), and precisely
therefore none is absolute: each office stands under the Torah and
remains testable and accountable. The king who does not lift his heart
and the prophet who is tested are the hinges.

\par\b\fs28 \u167?2. Peshat per block\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 2.1 Judges and justice (16:18\u8211?17:7)\b0\fs24\par


16:18\u8211?20 \u8212? judges in the gates; no bribe or partiality; tzedek
tzedek tirdof.
17:2\u8211?7 \u8212? idolatry is investigated carefully and requires two or
three witnesses.


\par\b\fs24 2.2 The high court (17:8\u8211?13)\b0\fs24\par


Too-difficult cases go to the place the LORD chooses, to the
priests and the judge; their ruling is binding.


\par\b\fs24 2.3 The king (17:14\u8211?20)\b0\fs24\par


God chooses him, from among your brothers; he does not multiply
horses (no return to Egypt), wives, or gold.
17:18\u8211?20 \u8212? he writes out this Torah with his own hand and reads
it for life, \u8216?that his heart may not be lifted up above his
brothers\u8217?. The hinge of the whole parashah: authority under the
Word.


\par\b\fs24 2.4 Priests and Levites (18:1\u8211?8)\b0\fs24\par


They have no inheritance; the LORD Himself is their inheritance.
Their support lies in God, not in possessions.


\par\b\fs24 2.5 No divination; blamelessness (18:9\u8211?14)\b0\fs24\par


Do not do as the nations (divination, sorcery, consulting the
dead); \u8216?you shall be blameless (tamim) before the LORD your
God\u8217? (18:13).
Weighing frame (application, not peshat-compulsion): the
distinction lies not in the act but in the directedness \u8212? at God, or at
the self. Embodied and creative gifts (writing that heals, music,
movement, breath) are good in themselves; it goes wrong where the self
becomes the center. God is the riverbed that carries; He made the body
to serve Him.


\par\b\fs24 2.6 The prophet like Moses (18:15\u8211?22)\b0\fs24\par


God raises a prophet like Moses to be heeded (18:15). The test:
\i \u8216?if the word does not come to pass, the LORD has not spoken it\u8217?\i0 
(18:22). Legitimate AND testable.

2.7 Cities of refuge, boundary stone, witnesses
(19)

Cities of refuge for unintentional manslaughter; do not move a
boundary stone (19:14); one witness is not enough (19:15); the false
witness receives what he intended for the other; proportionality
(19:21).


\par\b\fs24 2.8 Laws of war (20)\b0\fs24\par


The priest encourages; exemptions (new house, vineyard,
betrothed, the fearful); first an offer of peace; and the
spare-the-fruit-tree command (20:19\u8211?20).
The exemptions and the spared fruit tree protect life and
continuity, even in war \u8212? stewardship: the landscape is not the enemy.
Cf. the land on loan (Lev. 25:23) and the vineyard line Eden \u8594? Isa. 5 \u8594?
John 15.



\par\b\fs28 \u167?3. The two bearing axes and their relation\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 3.1 Source \u8212? all authority is derived\b0\fs24\par

The king, the prophet, the priest: none derives his place from
himself. God chooses, raises, appoints. And the illegitimate sources
(divination, the presumptuous prophet, other gods) are rejected
precisely as source. Legitimacy is received, not arrogated.

\par\b\fs24 3.2 Coherence \u8212? and therefore bounded\b0\fs24\par

Because the authority is derived, it is not absolute. The king does
not lift his heart and stands under the Torah; the prophet is tested by
the outcome; judgment is bounded by witnesses, investigation, and
proportionality. Power remains accountable \u8212? correctable.

\par\b\fs24 3.3 The relation \u8212? three hinges\b0\fs24\par


17:18\u8211?20 \u8212? God chooses the king (Source) AND binds him to writing
out and reading the Torah (Coherence). Source and limit in one
law.
18:15\u8211?22 \u8212? God raises the prophet (Source) AND sets the test:
does the word come true? (Coherence).
16:19\u8211?20 + 19:15\u8211?21 \u8212? pursue justice (16:20) through bounded
procedure: witnesses, investigation, proportionality.

Honest limit: this two-axis reading, too, can tempt to a too-neat
synthesis. The safeguard is that 17:18\u8211?20 and 18:15\u8211?22 are structural
hinges in the text itself, not brought by the reader.
\u167?4. Deepening: the limit as service to the
source
The four paradigm cases each show the same figure: authority that is
carried by the limit it accepts. The king who writes out the Torah
becomes not less king but more \u8212? he rules as a brother among brothers.
The prophet who lets himself be tested is not disempowered but
calibrated. The judge who requires two witnesses and judges
proportionally becomes not weaker but trustworthy. Even in war a limit
remains (the fruit tree, 20:19), and even at an unsolved murder
responsibility is not waved away but carried (21:6\u8211?7). The limit here is
no restriction of the authority; it is the form in which derived
authority becomes trustworthy.

\par\b\fs28 \u167?5. The directional test as horizon\b0\fs24\par

Beneath all this lies the one question \u8212? received or grasped \u8212? but
this week that is the ground, not the theme. Illegitimate,
self-arrogated authority (the presumptuous prophet 18:20, the king whose
heart lifts itself 17:20, the false witness 19:18) is the grasped form;
received, accountable, testable authority the received form. This time
the text pressed hardest on the Source\u8211?Coherence relation; the direction
is the floor beneath it. In the gospel that horizon becomes visible:
\i \u8216?I do not speak from Myself\u8217?\i0  (John 14:10) \u8212? the perfectly
received authority.

